From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CatPieLeaf <catpieleaf@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] USB: quirks: add USB_QUIRK_NO_LPM for ASIX AX88179/178A

The ASIX AX88179 USB 3.0 Gigabit Ethernet chip (0b95:1790) wedges on
the bus after running under sustained wired traffic: the driver logs
a spontaneous

  ax88179_178a 2-2:1.0 enp5s0f3u2: ax88179 - Link status is: 0

with no cable pull, and the interface never comes back. The xHC port
it is on subsequently fails to re-enable it:

  usb usb3-port4: Cannot enable. Maybe the USB cable is bad?
  usb usb3-port4: attempt power cycle
  usb usb3-port4: unable to enumerate USB device

The device only recovers once something forces a real bus/port reset
(e.g. `usbreset 0b95:1790`, or physically unplugging it), which
re-enumerates it from scratch and restores link.

This matches the same USB3 LPM (U1/U2) interaction that Realtek's
RTL8153-based gigabit dongles hit, already worked around via
USB_QUIRK_NO_LPM on 0bda:8153: SuperSpeed gigabit ethernet chips
autonegotiating link power states appear to fail to come back out of
U1/U2 during real traffic bursts and wedge until externally reset.
Disable LPM for the AX88179/178A the same way to keep it out of that
state.

---
 drivers/usb/core/quirks.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c b/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c
--- a/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/quirks.c
@@ -457,6 +457,9 @@ static const struct usb_device_id usb_quirk_list[] = {
 	/* ASUS TUF 4K PRO - BOS descriptor fetch hangs at SuperSpeed Plus */
 	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0b05, 0x1ab9), .driver_info = USB_QUIRK_NO_BOS },

+	/* ASIX AX88179/178A USB 3.0 Gigabit Ethernet - wedges under LPM */
+	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0b95, 0x1790), .driver_info = USB_QUIRK_NO_LPM },
+
 	/* Realtek Semiconductor Corp. Mass Storage Device (Multicard Reader)*/
 	{ USB_DEVICE(0x0bda, 0x0151), .driver_info = USB_QUIRK_CONFIG_INTF_STRINGS },

--
2.49.0
